   "source": [
    "## Example Walkthrough\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose we train an RNN on text. Input sentence: `“I love deep learning”`. We want the network to predict the next word.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step-by-Step Process\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Tokenization\n",
    "\n",
    "Words become tokens:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Word     | Token |\n",
    "| -------- | ----- |\n",
    "| I        | 1     |\n",
    "| love     | 2     |\n",
    "| deep     | 3     |\n",
    "| learning | 4     |\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Embedding\n",
    "\n",
    "Each word converts into a vector.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Word | Vector     |\n",
    "| ---- | ---------- |\n",
    "| I    | [0.1, 0.2] |\n",
    "| love | [0.7, 0.9] |\n",
    "| deep | [0.4, 0.6] |\n",
    "\n",
    "The vectors are initiliazed during training. It is random at start, then learned from data through repeated error correction.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Sequential Processing\n",
    "\n",
    "At each step:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Time 1\n",
    "\n",
    "Input:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_1 = \\text{\"I\"}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Hidden state:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "h_1 = f(x_1, h_0)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Time 2\n",
    "\n",
    "Input:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_2 = \\text{\"love\"}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now the network remembers “I”.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "h_2 = f(x_2, h_1)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Time 3\n",
    "\n",
    "Input:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_3 = \\text{\"deep\"}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now memory contains:\n",
    "\n",
    "* I\n",
    "* love\n",
    "* deep\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Predict Next Word\n",
    "\n",
    "The network outputs probabilities:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Word     | Probability |\n",
    "| -------- | ----------- |\n",
    "| learning | 0.92        |\n",
    "| neural   | 0.04        |\n",
    "| networks | 0.02        |\n",
    "\n",
    "Prediction: `learning`\n",
    "\n",
    "## Training an RNN\n",
    "\n",
    "RNNs are trained using:\n",
    "\n",
    "### Backpropagation Through Time (BPTT)\n",
    "\n",
    "It is an extension of backpropagation for sequences.\n",
    "\n",
    "The network:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Unrolls through time\n",
    "2. Computes loss at each step\n",
    "3. Propagates gradients backward through all time steps\n",
    "\n",
    "## Major Problems with Vanilla RNNs\n",
    "\n",
    "### Vanishing Gradient Problem\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradients become extremely small during backpropagation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Result:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Early information is forgotten\n",
    "* Long-term dependencies cannot be learned\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "> “I grew up in France ... I speak fluent French”\n",
    "\n",
    "The word “France” may be forgotten before reaching “French”.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Exploding Gradients\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradients become extremely large.\n",
    "\n",
    "Result:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Unstable training\n",
    "* Numerical overflow\n",
    "\n",
    "Solutions:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Gradient clipping\n",
    "\n",
    "## Solution\n",
    "\n",
    "To solve memory issues, researchers developed:\n",
    "\n",
    "### Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)\n",
    "\n",
    "LSTMs use gates:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Forget gate\n",
    "* Input gate\n",
    "* Output gate\n",
    "\n",
    "These gates control information flow.\n",
    "\n",
    "LSTMs can remember information for long periods.\n",
    "\n",
    "Used heavily in:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Translation\n",
    "* Speech systems\n",
    "* Text generation\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU)\n",
    "\n",
    "GRU is a simplified version of LSTM.\n",
    "\n",
    "Advantages:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Faster\n",
    "* Fewer parameters\n",
    "* Often similar performance\n",
    "\n",
    "## Charecterstics\n",
    "\n",
    "### Advantages\n",
    "\n",
    "| Advantage              | Description               |\n",
    "| ---------------------- | ------------------------- |\n",
    "| Handles sequences      | Designed for ordered data |\n",
    "| Shared weights         | Efficient parameter usage |\n",
    "| Variable-length inputs | Flexible                  |\n",
    "| Temporal memory        | Captures dependencies     |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "| Limitation             | Description                             |\n",
    "| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n",
    "| Vanishing gradients    | Hard to learn long-term patterns        |\n",
    "| Slow training          | Sequential computation                  |\n",
    "| Poor long-range memory | Basic RNN limitation                    |\n",
    "| Hard parallelization   | Cannot process all steps simultaneously |\n",
    "\n",
    "### RNN vs LSTM vs GRU\n",
    "\n",
    "| Feature           | RNN  | LSTM      | GRU       |\n",
    "| ----------------- | ---- | --------- | --------- |\n",
    "| Memory capability | Weak | Strong    | Strong    |\n",
    "| Complexity        | Low  | High      | Medium    |\n",
    "| Speed             | Fast | Slow      | Faster    |\n",
    "| Long sequences    | Poor | Excellent | Very good |\n",
    "\n",
    "### RNN vs Transformers\n",
    "\n",
    "Modern AI systems increasingly use Transformers instead of RNNs.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Google BERT\n",
    "* OpenAI GPT\n",
    "\n",
    "Why Transformers are preferred:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Transformers               | RNNs              |\n",
    "| -------------------------- | ----------------- |\n",
    "| Parallel processing        | Sequential        |\n",
    "| Better long-range learning | Weak long memory  |\n",
    "| Faster training            | Slower            |\n",
    "| Attention mechanism        | Hidden state only |\n",
    "\n",
    "Still, RNNs remain useful for:\n",
    "\n",
    "* small datasets\n",
    "* lightweight systems\n",
    "* streaming sequence data\n",
    "* embedded devices\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Long Short-Term Memory\n",
    "\n",
    "LSTM is a special kind of RNN introduced to solve long-term memory problems.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Main idea:** Instead of one hidden state, LSTM uses:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Cell state** → long-term memory\n",
    "2. **Hidden state** → short-term output\n",
    "\n",
    "LSTM carefully controls information using **gates**.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Intuition of LSTM**\n",
    "\n",
    "Think of LSTM as a smart memory system that can:\n",
    "\n",
    "* remember important information\n",
    "* forget useless information\n",
    "* update memory selectively\n",
    "\n",
    "This is done using gates.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why LSTM Works Better Than RNN**\n",
    "\n",
    "The cell state allows gradients to flow more easily.\n",
    "\n",
    "This reduces:\n",
    "\n",
    "* vanishing gradients\n",
    "* forgetting old information\n",
    "\n",
    "So LSTM can learn:\n",
    "\n",
    "* context\n",
    "* grammar\n",
    "* dependencies\n",
    "* time relationships\n",
    "\n",
    "over long sequences.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Components of LSTM\n",
    "\n",
    "An LSTM cell contains:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Component   | Purpose                  |\n",
    "| ----------- | ------------------------ |\n",
    "| Forget Gate | Removes unnecessary info |\n",
    "| Input Gate  | Adds new useful info     |\n",
    "| Cell State  | Long-term memory         |\n",
    "| Output Gate | Produces output          |\n",
    "\n",
    "## LSTM Architecture\n",
    "\n",
    "At time step $t$:\n",
    "\n",
    "Inputs:\n",
    "\n",
    "* current input $x_t$\n",
    "* previous hidden state $h_{t-1}$\n",
    "* previous cell state $C_{t-1}$\n",
    "\n",
    "Outputs:\n",
    "\n",
    "* new hidden state $h_t$\n",
    "* new cell state $C_t$\n",
    "\n",
    "![RNN Architecture](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook-old/removed/images/rnn/lstm.png)\n",
    "\n",
    "## LSTM Mathematics\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C_t = f_t \\odot C_{t-1} + i_t \\odot \\tilde{C}_t\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the heart of LSTM.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Forget Gate\n",
    "\n",
    "Decides what information to throw away.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f_t = \\sigma(W_f[h_{t-1},x_t] + b_f)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $\\sigma$ = sigmoid function\n",
    "* output range = 0 to 1\n",
    "\n",
    "It controls how much old memory survives.\n",
    "\n",
    "Interpretation:  \n",
    "\n",
    "* 0 → forget completely\n",
    "* 1 → keep completely\n",
    "\n",
    "### Input Gate\n",
    "\n",
    "Determines what new information to store.\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "i_t = \\sigma(W_i[h_{t-1},x_t] + b_i)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "* controls how much new info is written\n",
    "\n",
    "### Candidate memory\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\tilde{C}_t = \\tanh(W_c [h*{t-1}, x_t] + b_c)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "* new information proposal\n",
    "* can be positive or negative\n",
    "\n",
    "### Update Cell State\n",
    "\n",
    "Old memory updated with new memory.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "C_t = f_t \\odot C_{t-1} + i_t \\odot \\tilde{C}_t\n",
    "$$\n",
    "This is the key innovation of LSTM. The cell state acts like a conveyor belt carrying information through time with minimal modification.\n",
    "\n",
    "> old memory (scaled) + new memory (scaled)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Output Gate\n",
    "\n",
    "Determines what to output.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Output gate:**\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "o_t = \\sigma(W_o [h_{t-1}, x_t] + b_o)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "**Hidden state:**\n",
    "$$\n",
    "h_t = o_t \\odot \\tanh(C_t)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "* memory is stored in ($C_t$)\n",
    "* output is filtered version of it\n",
    "\n",
    "### The key mathematical intuition\n",
    "\n",
    "**Think of LSTM as a *soft memory controller***\n",
    "\n",
    "Each gate is a number between 0 and 1:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Gate  | Meaning                       |\n",
    "| ----- | ----------------------------- |\n",
    "| ($f_t$) | how much to forget old memory |\n",
    "| ($i_t$) | how much new info to write    |\n",
    "| ($o_t$) | how much memory to show       |\n",
    "\n",
    "So LSTM is basically:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **weighted addition + learned filtering**\n",
    "\n",
    "You can think of LSTM as:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\text{Memory}_t = (\\text{forget factor}) \\cdot \\text{old memory} + (\\text{input factor}) \\cdot \\text{new info}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "That’s it.\n",
    "\n",
    "Everything else is just learning how to set those factors.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Example Walkthrough\n",
    "\n",
    "Suppose sequence:\n",
    "```\n",
    "[\"I\", \"love\", \"deep\", \"learning\"]\n",
    "```\n",
    "Word embeddings become vectors:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Time Step 1\n",
    "\n",
    "Input:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_1 = I\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "LSTM initializes:\n",
    "\n",
    "* hidden state ($h_0$)\n",
    "* cell state ($C_0$)\n",
    "\n",
    "After processing:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $h_1$\n",
    "* $C_1$\n",
    "\n",
    "store information about “I”.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Time Step 2\n",
    "\n",
    "Input:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_2 = love\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "LSTM updates memory:\n",
    "\n",
    "* remembers subject “I”\n",
    "* adds emotion “love”\n",
    "\n",
    "Cell state now contains semantic meaning:\n",
    "\n",
    "> “I love”\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Time Step 3\n",
    "\n",
    "Input:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_3 = deep\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Updates context.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Time Step 4\n",
    "\n",
    "Input:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "x_4 = learning\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Now memory contains meaning of full phrase:\n",
    "\n",
    "> “I love deep learning”\n",
    "\n",
    "Final hidden state used for:\n",
    "\n",
    "* classification\n",
    "* translation\n",
    "* next-word prediction\n",
    "\n",
    "## Bidirectional LSTM\n",
    "\n",
    "Normal LSTM reads:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Left → Right\n",
    "\n",
    "Bidirectional LSTM reads:\n",
    "\n",
    "* Left → Right\n",
    "* Right → Left\n",
    "\n",
    "Useful in NLP because future words also provide context.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:\n",
    "\n",
    "> “bank”\n",
    "\n",
    "Meaning depends on later words:\n",
    "\n",
    "* river bank\n",
    "* bank account\n"
   ]
